# Files git must treat as binary.
#
# Git guesses binary-ness from NUL bytes in the first 8 KB. A ReportLab PDF whose streams are
# ASCII85-encoded has no NUL bytes there, so git classes it as TEXT: `git grep -I` then scans the
# compressed streams and matches credential- and email-shaped noise inside them. That is a false
# positive, and it made scripts/preflight_publish.sh fail on reports/test_results.pdf.
#
# Declaring them binary fixes the scan, and also stops git producing meaningless line diffs for them.
#
# NOTE the blind spot this creates: a PDF is no longer text-scanned by the preflight, so a real
# email address or secret inside one would not be caught by section 4. Any PDF added here must be
# checked by extracting its text first -- see reports/build_test_results_pdf.py, whose output is
# verified that way before committing.

*.pdf   binary
*.gz    binary
*.parquet binary
*.sqlite  binary
*.model   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
